}

{
 "summary": "1. Confirmed, for the same flag and user when the flag remains enabled. `bucketOf` hashes the same flag key and user ID deterministically (flags.ts:5-7; hash.ts:2-8). `evaluate` compares that bucket with the rollout percentage (flags.ts:18-22). A bucket below 10 is also below 20, so raising only the percentage cannot turn it off.\n2. Confirmed for a fetch rejection and a flag present in the retained snapshot. `refresh` catches a rejected fetch, calls `onError`, and does not replace the snapshot (flags.ts:28-35, flags.ts:36-37). `evaluate` reads that snapshot and uses the fallback only if the flag is absent (flags.ts:18-22). If `onError` throws, the refresh rejects, but the snapshot still remains unchanged.\n3. Confirmed for valid, ordered revisions. Each response is compared with the current snapshot after its fetch completes, and only a strictly newer revision replaces it (flags.ts:28-38). The service contract says revisions increase on every published change (types.ts:7-10). The comparison and assignment have no intervening await, so an older response arriving last is rejected; an older response arriving first is subsequently replaced by the newer one. A live runtime probe confirmed the newer-first ordering.\n4. Refuted as stated. `evaluate` is synchronous and reads only the in-memory snapshot (flags.ts:10, flags.ts:17-23), and its initial empty snapshot returns the fallback. However, refreshes can overlap (flags.ts:25-31): a second refresh can complete and populate the snapshot while the first call is still pending (flags.ts:36-38). A runtime probe observed `evaluate` return true rather than its false fallback before the first refresh completed.",
