}

{
 "summary": "1. Confirmed. For the same flag key and user ID, with the flag remaining enabled, increasing rollout from 10% to 20% cannot remove an included user. `hash.ts:2-8` deterministically hashes the input; `flags.ts:5-6` derives a bucket independently of rollout percentage. `flags.ts:21-22` compares that unchanged bucket against the percentage, so bucket < 10 implies bucket < 20. Fresh execution checked 10,000 users: all 1,023 selected at 10% remained selected at 20%. Changing enabled status is a separate operation, not rollout growth.\n2. Confirmed. A rejected fetch enters `flags.ts:31-34`, invokes onError, and exits without replacing the snapshot. The only replacement occurs on the successful path at `flags.ts:36-37`. Evaluation reads that retained snapshot at `flags.ts:19-22`; fallback applies only when the flag is absent. Fresh execution loaded an enabled flag, rejected the next refresh, and verified evaluation still returned true against a false fallback.\n3. Confirmed under the declared revision contract. `types.ts:7-10` defines revisions as increasing on each published change. Each refresh awaits its own fetch at `flags.ts:28-31`, then compares against the current shared snapshot at `flags.ts:36`, not a revision captured before awaiting. Comparison and assignment at `flags.ts:36-37` have no intervening await. If the older response arrives first, the newer replaces it; if the newer arrives first, the older is rejected. Fresh execution resolved overlapping revisions 1 and 2 in both orders and retained revision 2. This verifies client ordering, not whether an external service honors its revision contract.\n4. Confirmed. The snapshot starts with no flags at `flags.ts:10`. `evaluate` is synchronous and only reads memory, checks the flag, and computes a hash at `flags.ts:18-22` and `hash.ts:2-8`. Fetching occurs only in `refresh` at `flags.ts:28-31`. Before a refresh installs a snapshot, the own-property check and absent-flag branch return the supplied fallback at `flags.ts:19-20`. Fresh execution held the first refresh unresolved and verified both fallback values for ordinary and prototype-named keys; evaluation returned booleans immediately and made no additional fetch calls.",
 "files_modified": []
}

{
 "summary": "1. Confirmed: For the same flag key and user ID, `bucketOf` hashes the same input to the same bucket (`flags.ts:5-7`, `hash.ts:2-8`). For a flag that stays enabled, evaluation compares that bucket directly with the rollout percentage (`flags.ts:19-22`). Therefore a bucket below 10 is also below 20.\n2. Confirmed, with the existing unknown-flag behavior: A rejected fetch enters the catch, calls `onError`, and returns without assigning `snapshot` (`flags.ts:28-35`); only a successful newer fetch replaces it (`flags.ts:36-38`). Evaluation reads that retained snapshot (`flags.ts:18-22`). A flag absent from the retained snapshot still returns the caller's fallback (`flags.ts:19-20`). If `onError` throws, refresh rejects, but this code still does not replace the snapshot.\n3. Confirmed for ordered numeric revisions: Each resumed refresh compares its response against the current snapshot after the await, rejects an equal or older revision, and otherwise replaces the snapshot without an intervening await (`flags.ts:28-38`). The service contract says revisions increase with each published change (`types.ts:7-10`). A runtime probe completed revision 2 before revision 1 and retained revision 2. The injected `FetchFlags` type does not itself enforce valid revisions (`types.ts:13`), so this conclusion relies on the stated revision contract.\n4. Refuted as written: `evaluate` is synchronous and reads memory without calling the fetcher (`flags.ts:18-23`), and the initial empty snapshot returns fallback (`flags.ts:10`, `flags.ts:19-20`). However, overlapping refreshes are explicitly supported (`flags.ts:25-28`): a second refresh can complete and install a snapshot before the first call completes (`flags.ts:31`, `flags.ts:36-37`). A runtime probe observed a flag return true while the first refresh was still pending, after the second refresh completed.",
 "files_modified": []
}

{
 "summary": "1. Refuted. addMember emits inside the transaction callback after the queries (membership.ts:13-17). EventBus queues the notifier as a microtask (bus.ts:16-19); the notifier sends to the team's sockets (notifier.ts:9-13). Database awaits that callback before issuing COMMIT, and rolls back on failure (db.ts:23-28). A runtime probe using the actual classes observed client notification before COMMIT, followed by a failed COMMIT and ROLLBACK. Deferral does not enforce commit ordering or cancel notification on rollback.\n2. Confirmed, for calls on the same AuditLog instance. record reads the team's previous tail, starts its append only when that tail resolves, and stores a caught version of its own promise as the next tail (audit.ts:8,12-16). A failed append still rejects its own returned promise, but the caught tail allows the next append to run. A runtime probe held the first append pending, then rejected it; the second started only afterward.\n3. Confirmed. Both membership methods await the transaction before calling audit.record (membership.ts:13-18,23-31). Database propagates transaction errors after attempting rollback (db.ts:19-28), so a thrown transaction skips those audit calls. In the failed-commit probe, no audit append occurred. This does not imply notification is suppressed: claim 1 shows it is not.\n4. Confirmed for a DELETE reporting rowCount zero. removeMember returns false from the transaction callback without updating the count (membership.ts:23-27), then returns false before emit and audit.record (membership.ts:28-32). A runtime probe returned rowCount zero and observed only BEGIN, DELETE, and COMMIT, with no event or audit append.",
