{
 "summary": "1. Confirmed, while the flag remains enabled and its key and user ID stay the same. `flags.ts:5-6` derives the bucket only from the flag key and user ID; `hash.ts:2-8` computes it deterministically. `flags.ts:21-22` compares that same bucket with the rollout percentage, so a bucket below 10 is also below 20. A runtime probe confirmed a bucket-8 user remained enabled after 10% grew to 20%.\n2. Confirmed for a flag in the retained snapshot. `flags.ts:29-35` catches a rejected fetch without assigning a new snapshot, and `flags.ts:17-22` evaluates from the existing snapshot. An unknown flag still returns its caller-provided fallback (`flags.ts:19-20`), including after a failed refresh. A runtime probe confirmed both cases. If the error callback itself throws, refresh rejects instead of returning false, but this path still does not replace the snapshot (`flags.ts:32-35`).\n3. Confirmed for flag sets obeying the revision contract. Revisions increase on every published change (`types.ts:7-10`); after each awaited fetch, `flags.ts:31,36-38` compares against the current snapshot and assigns synchronously only if the response is newer. If the newer response arrives first, the older one is rejected; if the older arrives first, the newer replaces it. Runtime probes exercised both response orders. The HTTP adapter only checks that revision has JavaScript type number (`http-flags.ts:9-13`), so this conclusion depends on the stated revision contract rather than complete response validation.\n4. Confirmed for the initial snapshot, before any refresh has completed. The constructor initializes an empty in-memory flag set (`flags.ts:9-15`), and `evaluate` synchronously reads it and returns the fallback for absent keys (`flags.ts:17-23`); it neither calls the fetch function nor awaits anything. Network fetching happens separately in `refresh` (`flags.ts:28-31`). A runtime probe held refreshes pending and observed both caller fallback values. If a later-started overlapping refresh completes first, its snapshot becomes available even while the original call is pending (`flags.ts:36-38`).",
