{
 "summary": "1. Confirmed: For one limiter instance, both cold acquires share the same in-flight promise (limiter.ts:20,29-31,39-40). The load installs and returns the same array (limiter.ts:32-36). Each continuation checks and pushes synchronously before awaiting save (limiter.ts:21-25), so the second sees the first slot consumed and returns false. A runtime probe returned [true, false] with one load and one save. This relies on the stated single-process ownership (limiter.ts:3-6).\n2. Confirmed: On each acquire, the limiter reads the clock, removes oldest timestamps at or before now minus windowMs, and only then checks the count (limiter.ts:20-23). The store contract specifies oldest-first timestamps (types.ts:3-4). No timer is needed; a runtime probe denied at time 100 and allowed at time 150 when timestamp 50 expired.\n3. Confirmed: After pruning, a full window returns false before either the push or save (limiter.ts:22-25). A runtime probe observed denial with zero writes. Pruning can still mutate the in-memory array.\n4. Confirmed: A rejected load skips the success handler that populates windows (limiter.ts:32-36), while finally deletes the in-flight entry (limiter.ts:38-40). The next acquire therefore misses both caches and calls load again (limiter.ts:20,29-33). A runtime probe observed first-load rejection followed by a successful retry and two loads.",
