}

{
 "summary": "1. Refuted. addMember emits inside the transaction after the INSERT and UPDATE (membership.ts:13-17). The bus queues the listener as a microtask (bus.ts:16-19); the notifier sends the event to team sockets (notifier.ts:9-13). Database.transaction awaits the callback before issuing COMMIT, and rolls back on a commit error (db.ts:23-28). A Bun probe using the actual classes and a controlled connection observed member.added before COMMIT, followed by failed COMMIT and ROLLBACK. Thus a client can receive the event for a rolled-back change.\n2. Confirmed for calls on the same AuditLog instance. Each record reads the team's prior tail and schedules its sink append after that tail (audit.ts:8,12-14). It stores a caught version of the new promise as the next tail, so a rejection does not block the next append, while returning the original rejection to the caller (audit.ts:15-16). A Bun probe held the first append open, rejected it, and observed the second append start only after the first failed. Separate AuditLog instances do not share tails.\n3. Confirmed. addMember calls audit.record only after its awaited transaction resolves (membership.ts:12-18). removeMember likewise awaits the transaction before it can call audit.record (membership.ts:22-31). If either transaction rejects, execution does not reach that change's audit call; Database.transaction propagates errors after attempting rollback (db.ts:23-28).\n4. Uncertain. If DELETE returns rowCount 0, the transaction callback returns false before updating the team (membership.ts:23-27), and removeMember returns before emitting or recording (membership.ts:28-31). A Bun probe confirmed this path with a controlled connection. The supplied project only declares the connection and rowCount interfaces (db.ts:1-7); it provides no database driver or implementation establishing that an absent member produces rowCount 0. That boundary evidence is missing.",
