{
 "summary": "1. Confirmed, for concurrent calls on the same limiter instance: both missing-key calls enter hydrate (limiter.ts:19-20). The second receives the first call's pending promise (limiter.ts:29-31, 38-40); both resume with the same array stored by the load handler (limiter.ts:32-36). Each checks and pushes without an intervening await (limiter.ts:21-25), so the first takes the last slot and the second returns false. A Bun probe returned [true, false] with one load and one save.\n2. Confirmed: the store contract supplies timestamps oldest first (types.ts:2-4). Each acquisition reads the clock, shifts all leading timestamps at or before now minus windowMs, and only then compares the count (limiter.ts:20-23). Thus a later acquisition reclaims expired slots without a timer. A Bun probe advanced the clock and observed a new allowance.\n3. Confirmed: after expiration cleanup, the full-window branch returns false before the timestamp push and store.save (limiter.ts:22-25). Cleanup can mutate the in-memory array, but denial adds no timestamp and invokes no save. The concurrent Bun probe observed one save for one allowed and one denied call.\n4. Confirmed: the window is cached only in the fulfillment handler (limiter.ts:32-36), while finally deletes the pending promise on rejection as well (limiter.ts:38-40). The rejected acquire propagates the error from its awaited hydrate (limiter.ts:19-20). A later call finds neither cached window nor pending promise and calls load again (limiter.ts:20, 29-33). A Bun probe observed two load attempts and a successful second acquire.",
 "files_modified": []
}

{
 "summary": "1. Refuted. `authorize` waits on the cache load (`sessions.ts:24-30`); `revoke` awaits the store update and invalidates (`sessions.ts:34-36`), but invalidation neither removes the inflight promise nor prevents its result from being stored (`cache.ts:25-28,32-44`). The load records the current generation without comparing it against the generation at load start (`cache.ts:39-40,47-48`). A controlled Bun probe resolved an old, unrevoked read after revoke completed: both a later authorize already waiting on that read and a subsequent cache hit returned the session.\n2. Refuted. `get` invokes `loadAndStore` before registering its promise (`cache.ts:25-29`), and `loadAndStore` synchronously invokes the loader before its first suspension (`cache.ts:37-39`). The loader calls the injected store (`sessions.ts:27`; `types.ts:8-11`), which can synchronously reenter `authorize` before returning its promise. A Bun probe with such a store observed two `find` calls. Ordinary non-reentrant concurrent callers do share the registered promise.\n3. Confirmed with respect to the injected clock. SessionAuth supplies a 60,000 ms TTL and the same clock (`sessions.ts:4,15-20`). A successful load sets expiry to clock.now() plus TTL (`cache.ts:37-41`); every entry hit requires expiry strictly greater than clock.now() (`cache.ts:22-24`). A Bun probe observed a hit at 59,999 ms and a reload at 60,000 ms. This does not establish a wall-clock guarantee if the injected clock moves backward; its interface specifies no monotonicity (`types.ts:14-16`).\n4. Confirmed for a returned promise that rejects. The rejection at `await load()` skips the entry write, and `finally` removes the inflight promise (`cache.ts:37-44`); a later `get` therefore starts another load (`cache.ts:22-29`). A Bun probe observed two store calls after two rejected promises. Distinct case: a store method that throws synchronously causes `finally` to run before `get` registers the rejected promise, leaving that promise in inflight; a separate probe observed only one call (`cache.ts:27-28,37-44`). The provided SQL store uses an async `find` (`sql-store.ts:19-23`), so its failures return rejected promises rather than synchronous throws.",
 "files_modified": []
}

{
 "summary": "1. Refuted. authorize waits on cache.get and store.find (sessions.ts:24-27). revoke awaits markRevoked, then deletes the cache entry and increments its generation (sessions.ts:34-36; cache.ts:32-35). It does not remove the inflight promise, and loadAndStore neither captures nor compares the earlier generation before storing and returning the result (cache.ts:25-29, 37-43). A later authorize can join the old load and receive its unrevoked session after revoke resolves. A Bun probe reproduced that sequence and returned the session to both calls.\n2. Refuted. get calls loadAndStore before registering its promise (cache.ts:25-29); loadAndStore invokes load synchronously before its first await (cache.ts:37-39), and authorize's loader calls store.find (sessions.ts:24-27). A synchronously reentrant store.find can call authorize again during that gap, causing a second find. A Bun probe observed two calls. Ordinary non-reentrant concurrent misses do share the registered promise.\n3. Confirmed for the injected clock's time. SessionAuth supplies a 60,000 ms TTL (sessions.ts:4, 19). A cache entry records clock.now() plus TTL after the load (cache.ts:39-40); every cached hit requires expiresAt > clock.now() (cache.ts:22-24), so equality is expired. A Bun probe observed a hit at 59,999 ms and a reload at 60,000 ms. The code does not establish a monotonic or wall-clock guarantee for the injected clock (types.ts:14-16).\n4. Confirmed for a promise returned by store.find that rejects. The rejected await skips the entry write, and finally deletes the inflight promise (cache.ts:37-44); the next miss invokes the loader again (cache.ts:22-29; sessions.ts:27). A Bun probe observed two find calls after two rejected promises. Distinct boundary: a synchronous throw from a custom find causes finally to delete before get registers the rejected promise, leaving it cached in inflight (cache.ts:27-28, 37-44); a probe observed only one find call. SqlSessionStore.find is async (sql-store.ts:19-23).",
 "files_modified": []
}

{
 "summary": "1. CONFIRMED, for concurrent calls on the same limiter instance. Both miss `windows` and await hydration (`limiter.ts:19-20`); the second reuses the pending promise (`limiter.ts:29-31,38-40`). The load stores and returns the same array (`limiter.ts:32-36`). Each resumed call checks and mutates that array without an intervening await (`limiter.ts:21-25`), so the first fills the last slot and the second returns false. A Bun probe observed one load, one allowed request, and one denial. The single-process ownership statement is a documented premise, not independently verified (`limiter.ts:3-6`).\n2. CONFIRMED, under the store's oldest-first contract (`types.ts:2-4`). On each acquire, the limiter reads the clock, shifts timestamps at or before `now - windowMs`, and only then checks the count (`limiter.ts:20-23`). No timer is needed: a later acquire performs the pruning. A Bun probe confirmed a slot returns exactly at the expiration boundary.\n3. CONFIRMED. After pruning, a full window returns false before the push and save (`limiter.ts:22-25`), so the denied request adds no timestamp and causes no store write. Pruning itself can remove expired timestamps. A Bun probe confirmed the denied call did not increase the write count.\n4. CONFIRMED. A rejected load cannot execute the fulfillment callback that populates `windows` (`limiter.ts:32-36`); `finally` removes the pending hydration on rejection (`limiter.ts:38`). The next acquire therefore misses the window and starts a new load (`limiter.ts:20,29-33`). The KV adapter propagates get and parse failures (`kv-window-store.ts:7-10`). A Bun probe made the first KV get fail and confirmed the next acquire performed a second get and succeeded.",
 "files_modified": []
}

{
 "summary": "1. **Refuted.** `authorize` loads by session ID through the cache (`sessions.ts:24-27`). If that load is pending when `revoke` completes, `revoke` marks the row and invalidates the entry (`sessions.ts:34-36`), but invalidation does not remove the in-flight promise (`cache.ts:25-26,32-35`). A later authorize can join that promise, and its stale result is unconditionally cached with the current generation (`cache.ts:37-43`). The generation is never compared with a captured pre-invalidation value. Both the later caller and subsequent cache hits can return the unrevoked session (`sessions.ts:27-30`). A runtime probe reproduced both returns after revoke resolved.\n2. **Refuted.** `get` invokes `loadAndStore` before registering its promise (`cache.ts:25-29`), and `loadAndStore` synchronously calls the loader before awaiting it (`cache.ts:37-39`). The loader calls `store.find` (`sessions.ts:27`); the provided SQL store synchronously calls the injected database query before its own await (`sql-store.ts:19-20`, `sql-store.ts:3-6`). That callback can reenter `authorize` for the same ID while no in-flight entry exists. A runtime probe using `SqlSessionStore` and a reentrant database query produced two queries. Without synchronous reentrancy, ordinary concurrent callers share the registered promise.\n3. **Confirmed relative to the injected clock.** `SessionAuth` sets a 60,000 ms cache TTL (`sessions.ts:4,15-20`), and a successful load sets expiry to `clock.now() + ttlMs` (`cache.ts:37-41`). Every entry hit requires `expiresAt > clock.now()` (`cache.ts:22-24`), so equality or a later clock reading causes a reload. A runtime probe observed a hit at 59,999 and a reload at 60,000. The `Clock` interface does not guarantee a monotonic clock (`types.ts:14-16`), so this does not independently guarantee 60 seconds of real elapsed time if that clock stalls or moves backwards.\n4. **Confirmed for a returned promise that rejects.** `loadAndStore` awaits the loader before setting the entry, so a rejected promise skips the entry write and executes the `finally` deletion (`cache.ts:37-44`). Because that rejection resumes after `get` registers the in-flight promise (`cache.ts:27-29`), the next call starts a new load (`cache.ts:22-27`). A runtime probe observed two calls for two successive rejected promises. Distinct edge case: a custom `SessionStore.find` may synchronously throw before returning a promise (`types.ts:8-11`); in that case `finally` runs before the rejected `loadAndStore` promise is registered, leaving that promise in-flight. A runtime probe observed only one find call across two authorizations. The provided `SqlSessionStore.find` is async (`sql-store.ts:19-23`), so its thrown errors become promise rejections instead.",
 "files_modified": []
